A||N||6||kmunson56||Installing '88' suspension in '87'||||5||
Z||000000||kmunson56||10-16-2000||09:39 PM||||Hi! This is my first time posting a question. I just got the front and rear suspension out of an '88' coupe at a U-pull-n-save yard last weekend. I plan to put it in my '87'GT. I know there is going to be some modifications required. On the rear it seems like I will only need to change the motor mount bracket on the engine. The front appears to need some modification where the rear mount for the frame locates to the body. I obtaned the brake lines and coolant pipes from front to rear when I got the suspension. My question is has anyone else done this swap and have I missed anything ?<P>Thanks,<BR>Kris A. Munson,Sr.<BR> '87'GT<BR> '88' Formula||||reg||5
Z||000001||gixxer||10-16-2000||10:35 PM||||I know "Will" was doing at least the rear end, and earlier, another fellow actually did the front. You may want to perform a search and find the relevant posts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ghia||10-16-2000||10:46 PM||||Kris,<P>I would talk to Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory.  I was going to do the same thing on my 87GT and I talked to Ed about it and changed my mind.  The rear is fairly easy, but the front is going to require an awful lot of work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierov8||10-17-2000||01:21 AM||||Kris,<P>Firsties:  Welcome to the Forum -- much info to be gained here from all.<P>The short answer is:  half of your suspension swap will be easy, half will be very hard - others have tried and failed, or at least decided to quit before they butchered their car past the point of no return.<P>Now on to the long answer:<P>The rear suspension is a piece of cake.  The 88 cradle bolts in where the pre-88 one did.  The only difficulty you have ahead of you in this area is with the struts.  In order to fit struts to the car, you need an adapter from Ryane (now defunct) Motorsports, or find a kind soul to measure/diagram/photograph a set for you.  An adapter is needed at the top of the strut tower because the top of the strut is "forward" (towards front of car) and "inward" (toward the center of the car) with respect to the 87 strut.  Further, this adapter needs to mount a coilover rather than a 88 or pre-88 strut.  The reason for the coilover is that the strut is moved to a "corner" of the strut tower, there is not enough room in the tower to mount a factory spring -- it will contact the side of the strut tower.  Been there, done this -- I have an 88 cradle and susp. in my 86 SE/GT/V8.<P>I know the above has to sound confusing -- if so, do this:  mount the cradle in the car.  Mount up the three links and the cast knuckle.  If you draw an imaginary line through the center of the knuckle, you will see where the center of the coilover strut will want to be.<P>Now -- on to the front suspension.  I have heard on-net from several people that it is insanely difficult to mount an 88 front suspension to a pre-88 car.  Still, I looked into it -- it won't work.  What happened on the 88 cars is that the main chassis up front changed -- the frame rails were brought more towards the center of the car.  This allowed for the longer upper/lower control arms of the 88's.  This in turn changed the mount points for the crossmember.  To elaborate on an earlier post, I have seen where Ed Parks has said that it is easier to chop the entire frame off the front of an 88 and weld it onto the pre-88 car.<P>All is not lost - sell/part out that 88 front suspension and buy yourself some lowering springs, poly bushings, and Konis for the front.  You will have a front suspension that performs as well (within 9.5 tenths) of the 88 suspension.  A well-heeled track driver may notice the difference on-track, but the average/above average driver won't notice anything on the street or in an autocross.  <P>Best thing you are capable of doing is swapping in the 88 cradle in the rear -- you eliminate the bump steer.  Also add poly in the rear as well.<P>Hope this helps -- just trying to help you avoid cutting up the car and irreparably damaging it.<P>-jeff d<BR>Fort Worth, TX||||reg||9
Z||000004||william_thorniii||10-17-2000||07:44 AM||||Kris,<P>I just so happen to have a set of Ryanne's 88 rear conversion plates in my garage.  If you get fiero56 to take a picture for me, would be happy to get you dimensions... but I am not sure of the legality of such matters.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-26-2000||12:59 PM||||Kris,<P>I sent you the information you requested yesterday to both of your e-mail accounts and haven't heard anything... you still out there?  Bringing this back to the top so you might see it and fill me in on progress or what information you still need.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000006||Will||10-26-2000||03:02 PM||||Check this out:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004275.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004275.html</A> <P>It details how to transplant an '88 front into an '86.<P>If you don't want to go to that much trouble, I would be willing to buy that front suspension, brake lines, etc. from you for a reasonable price.||||reg||1
